Description: Fix CVE-2026-24425 - Twig sandbox bypass in arrow-accepting filters
 The runtime check in CoreExtension::checkArrowInSandbox that rejects non-Closure
 callbacks passed to arrow-accepting filters (sort, filter, map, reduce, find,
 has some, has every) previously called SandboxExtension::isSandboxed() with no
 arguments. When the sandbox was enabled per-template via a SourcePolicyInterface
 (rather than globally), isSandboxed() returned false because it had no Source to
 consult, so user-controlled templates could pass arbitrary PHP callables to
 these filters, enabling arbitrary PHP code execution.
 .
 This backport fixes the vulnerability by making checkArrowInSandbox walk the
 PHP debug backtrace to recover the calling Template's Source and forward it to
 SandboxExtension::isSandboxed($source), so source-policy sandboxing is
 correctly consulted. The signature of checkArrowInSandbox and all six call
 sites (sort/filter/find/map/reduce/arraySome/arrayEvery) remain unchanged, so
 the fix is fully BC-compatible with v3.11.3.
 .
 The upstream fix (twig/twig 3.26.0) refactors this through a new
 needs_is_sandboxed callable option threaded through AbstractTwigCallable /
 TwigCallableInterface and a new twig_resolve_caller_source() helper. That
 architecture does not exist in v3.11.3 (15 minor versions behind), so this
 backport applies approach (a) from the complexity report: inline Source
 resolution inside checkArrowInSandbox using debug_backtrace, matching the
 behavior of the upstream twig_resolve_caller_source() helper.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/twigphp/Twig/commit/d0579ededc67241ebddfbe5b019426933ec7b1b7
Bug: https://github.com/twigphp/Twig/security/advisories (CVE-2026-24425)
Author: Backport for twig/twig v3.11.3
Last-Update: 2026-07-13

